# Secrets — never commit.
#
# Deployed builds read these from the Netlify environment; only local
# `netlify dev` needs a file on disk. That file holds the keys that actually
# matter — SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY (bypasses every RLS policy in supabase/),
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, RESEND_API_KEY, ACCULYNX_IMPORT_SECRET.
#
# A .env was committed once already (5519ea2, untracked again in 80af63c). That
# one happened to hold only VITE_SUPABASE_URL and VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY, both
# public by design and both shipped in the client bundle anyway, so nothing
# leaked. The next one would not be so lucky. Anything committed here is in the
# history on two GitHub remotes and has to be treated as burned.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
# .env.ios holds no secrets: one line, VITE_APP_PLATFORM=ios. It has to be
# committed or the App Store build is not reproducible from a clean checkout,
# which is exactly what a CI macOS runner starts from. See src/utils/platform.js.
!.env.ios

# Local Netlify folder
.netlify

# Dependencies — never commit installed packages
node_modules/

# Personal projects living in Documents that should stay out of this repo
vuln-scanner/
# Anchored with a leading slash on purpose. As a bare "App" this matched a
# directory of that name at ANY depth, which silently swallowed
# "MRR Production/ios/App" — the entire generated Xcode project, including
# project.pbxproj, Info.plist and AppDelegate.swift. Nothing warns you: the files
# sit on disk, `git add -A` skips them, and a CI macOS runner clones a repo with
# no iOS project in it. The leading slash confines this to Documents/App.
/App
Baseline/
Home-SOC-Project/
Internship/
Keylogger/
NoteTaker/
phishing-url-detector/
Trucking/
Vitaal/

# Archives
*.zip
